.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
.Dd April 22, 2026
.Dt MKIT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mkit
.Nd decentralized, content-addressed version control with cryptographic attribution
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl C Ar path
.Op Fl c Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl Fl no-pager
.Ar command
.Op Ar args ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a git-like command-line tool that produces signed, content-addressed
objects and exchanges them over pluggable transports
.Pq Cm mkit+file , Cm mkit+https , Cm mkit+s3 , Cm mkit+ssh .
Every commit is Ed25519-signed and identified by its BLAKE3 hash, so
chains of objects are self-verifying independent of the store they live
in.
.Pp
The on-disk layout under
.Pa .mkit/
is deliberately similar to
.Pa .git/ :
objects are stored under
.Pa .mkit/objects/ ,
refs under
.Pa .mkit/refs/ ,
and the current branch is recorded in
.Pa .mkit/HEAD .
A user-level config at
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mkit/config
provides cross-repo defaults; repo-local values in
.Pa .mkit/config
win over it for ordinary keys, but security-sensitive keys
.Pq Cm signing_key , Cm user.identity , Cm signer , Cm key.* , Cm attest.* , Cm ssh.* , No and Cm trusted_remote_endpoint
are user-scoped only and are ignored if set repo-locally.
.Sh GLOBAL OPTIONS
Parsed before
.Ar command
(so they apply to repository discovery), like git:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl C Ar path
Run as if
.Nm
were started in
.Ar path .
Repeatable; relative paths resolve against the prior
.Fl C .
.It Fl c Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
Set a config value for this invocation only. It is applied to the
effective config (never written to disk) and flows through the same
enforcement as a per-repo config file: security-sensitive keys are
refused and dangerous
.Cm core.*
keys are dropped, so
.Fl c
cannot spoof the signed author or redirect trust.
.It Fl Fl no-pager , Fl P , Fl Fl paginate
Accepted no-ops:
.Nm
never paginates.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
The full list of subcommands is printed by
.Nm mkit Fl Fl help .
The highlights:
.Bl -tag -width "cherry-pick"
.It Cm init
Create a new
.Nm
repository in the current directory.
.It Cm add Oo Fl A | Fl Fl all Oc Oo Fl u | Fl Fl update Oc Oo Fl f Oc Oo Fl p Oc Ar path ...
Stage files for the next commit. Respects
.Pa .gitignore
and
.Pa .mkitignore .
Multiple pathspecs may be given;
.Ql .
stages every non-ignored file under the current directory.
.Fl A
or
.Fl Fl all
stages every worktree change including deletions of tracked files (takes no
path arguments).
.Fl u
or
.Fl Fl update
restages only already-tracked files \(em updating modified ones and recording
deletions \(em without adding untracked paths (takes no path arguments).
.Fl A
and
.Fl u
are mutually exclusive.
.Fl f
or
.Fl Fl force
stages an explicitly-named ignored path (git refuses these without it;
already-tracked paths are never subject to ignore).
.Fl p
or
.Fl Fl patch
interactively presents each hunk between the staged (or HEAD) blob and the
worktree file, staging only the ones you accept: per hunk,
.Ql y
stages it,
.Ql n
skips it,
.Ql a
stages it and all later hunks in the file,
.Ql d
skips it and all later hunks, and
.Ql q
quits (keeping hunks already chosen). It requires explicit path arguments,
is incompatible with
.Fl A
and
.Fl u ,
and operates on regular text files only: a binary file is skipped with a
message (the command still succeeds), while symlinks and directories are
refused. An explicitly-named ignored path is refused unless
.Fl f
(like plain
.Cm add ) ,
and a path escaping the repository through a symlinked parent directory is
refused.
.It Cm rm Oo Fl Fl cached Oc Oo Fl r | Fl Fl recursive Oc Oo Fl f | Fl Fl force Oc Ar path ...
Remove paths and stage the deletion for the next commit. By default this
deletes the worktree file(s) and prunes now-empty parent directories.
Multiple pathspecs are accepted.
.Fl Fl cached
stages the removal only, keeping the worktree file(s).
.Fl r
or
.Fl Fl recursive
is required to remove a directory and everything under it.
.Fl f
or
.Fl Fl force
removes files even when their worktree content has diverged from the staged
blob; without it,
.Cm rm
refuses to destroy a locally-modified tracked file.
.It Cm mv Oo Fl f | Fl Fl force Oc Ar source ... Ar dest
Move or rename a tracked path and stage the change.
.Cm mv Ar src Ar dst
renames
.Ar src
to
.Ar dst ,
or moves it into
.Ar dst
when that is an existing directory; with more than one source,
.Ar dest
must be an existing directory. The worktree file is moved and the index
updated (source staged as removed, destination staged with the source's blob
and mode). mkit has no rename detection, so
.Cm status
reports the move as a delete plus an add rather than git's
.Ql R .
.Fl f
or
.Fl Fl force
overwrites an existing destination; without it,
.Cm mv
refuses to clobber one. A missing or untracked source is refused.
A
.Ar source
may also be a directory:
.Cm mv Ar dir Ar newdir
renames it and
.Cm mv Ar dir Ar existing-dir
moves it inside, as
.Pa existing-dir/dir .
The directory is renamed in one filesystem operation, so untracked files
within it travel along (like
.Xr git 1 Ns 's
.Cm mv ) ,
and each tracked file beneath it is restaged at its new path. A directory
move refuses a pre-existing destination even with
.Fl f
(recursively removing it would strand tracked files and destroy untracked
ones), rejects overlapping sources
.Pq Cm mv Ar dir Ar dir/file Ar dest ,
and refuses moving a directory into itself.
.It Cm restore Oo Fl Fl staged Oc Oo Fl Fl worktree Oc Oo Fl Fl source Ar rev Oc Oo Fl f | Fl Fl force Oc Ar path ...
Restore path(s) in the worktree and/or unstage them. With no flag (or
.Fl Fl worktree ) ,
the worktree file(s) are rewritten from the staged index content,
discarding un-staged worktree edits.
.Fl Fl staged
instead reverts the index entry for each path to its
.Cm HEAD
version (unstaging it) while leaving the worktree file untouched;
.Fl Fl staged Fl Fl worktree
does both. A directory path restores every tracked entry at or below it.
.Fl Fl source Ar rev
takes the restored content from
.Ar rev Ns 's
tree (resolved as a branch, tag,
.Cm HEAD ,
full or short hash, or
.Cm HEAD~ Ns Ar n Ns / Ns Cm ^
navigation) instead of the index/HEAD default. The worktree restore
refuses to overwrite a file whose content diverges from the staged blob;
.Fl f
or
.Fl Fl force
discards that un-staged edit. A worktree restore never removes neighbouring
files \(em it only rewrites the named tracked paths.
.It Cm reset Oo Fl Fl soft | Fl Fl mixed | Fl Fl hard Oc Oo Fl f Oc Op Ar commit
Move
.Cm HEAD
.Pq and the current branch
to
.Ar commit
.Pq default Cm HEAD ,
resolved through the shared revision grammar (branch, tag,
.Cm HEAD ,
full or short hash,
.Cm HEAD~ Ns Ar n Ns / Ns Cm ^ ) .
.Fl Fl soft
moves
.Cm HEAD
only, leaving the index and worktree untouched.
.Fl Fl mixed
.Pq the default
also resets the index to the target commit's tree while leaving the
worktree.
.Fl Fl hard
additionally resets the worktree to the target tree \(em overwriting
tracked-file changes and removing tracked files the target drops, while
keeping untracked files (like git). As a safety divergence,
.Fl Fl hard
refuses to discard locally-modified or staged content unless
.Fl f
.Pq Fl Fl force
is given;
.Xr git 1 Cm reset Fl Fl hard
discards silently.
.It Cm clean Oo Fl n Oc Oo Fl f Oc Oo Fl d Oc Oo Fl x | Fl X Oc Op Ar path ...
Remove untracked files from the worktree. Destructive, so it refuses to
delete without
.Fl f
.Pq matching git's Cm clean.requireForce ;
.Fl n
previews with
.Ql Would remove <path>
lines. Without
.Fl d ,
untracked directories are left alone;
.Fl d
removes them too. Ignored files are kept unless
.Fl x
.Pq remove ignored too
or
.Fl X
.Pq remove only ignored .
Ignore matching uses mkit's
.Pa .mkitignore
matcher (basename/root-only subset).
.It Cm hash Ar file
Hash
.Ar file
as a blob, store it under
.Pa .mkit/objects/ ,
and print the resulting BLAKE3 hash on stdout.
.It Cm cat Ar hash
Decode and print the object identified by
.Ar hash
on stdout.
.It Cm cat-file Bro Fl t | Fl s | Fl p Brc Ar object | Cm cat-file Fl Fl batch
Inspect a stored object (read-only plumbing, like
.Xr git 1 Cm cat-file ) .
.Fl t
prints the type
.Pq Cm blob Ns / Ns Cm tree Ns / Ns Cm commit Ns / Ns Cm tag Ns ; mkit's Cm remix No is the one non-git type ,
.Fl s
the size (content byte length for blobs, mkit's serialized size for
trees/commits), and
.Fl p
pretty-prints (blob bytes, a tree as
.Ql <mode> <type> <hash>\et<name>
lines, or a commit/tag summary).
.Fl Fl batch
reads object names from stdin (one per line) and emits a
.Ql <hash> <type> <size>
header plus the content for each
.Pq Ql <name> missing No for unknown objects .
.Ar object
resolves through the shared revspec grammar.
.It Cm show Oo Fl Fl stat Oc Op Ar object ...
Display objects, like
.Xr git 1 Cm show ,
defaulting to
.Cm HEAD .
A commit or remix prints a header
.Pq Cm commit Ar hash , Cm Author , Cm Date , indented message
followed by the unified diff against its first parent (byte-identical to
.Cm mkit diff Ar parent Ar commit ) ;
a tag prints its header and then the peeled target; a tree is listed as
.Ql <mode> <type> <hash>\et<name>
lines; a blob prints its raw contents. As with
.Cm log ,
the commit/tag header carries mkit's signed
.Ar Identity
and 64-hex BLAKE3 ids, so those lines diverge from git's
.Ql Author: Name <email>
/ 40-hex form; the diff body, tree listing, and blob output match git.
.Fl Fl stat
prints a diffstat
.Pq as in Cm mkit diff Fl Fl stat
instead of the patch for commits and remixes.
.It Cm tree
Snapshot the working directory as a tree object and print the tree hash.
Honors
.Pa .mkitignore
the same way
.Cm add
does.
.It Cm ls-tree Oo Fl r Oc Oo Fl z Oc Ar tree-ish Op Ar path ...
List a tree's entries as
.Ql <mode> <type> <hash>\et<name>
(git octal modes; 64-hex BLAKE3 hash).
.Fl r
recurses (leaf blobs with full paths, no tree lines);
.Fl z
NUL-terminates records with raw paths.
.It Cm ls-files Oo Fl s Oc Oo Fl z Oc Oo Fl Fl others Oc Oo Fl Fl ignored Oc Op Fl Fl exclude-standard
List files in the index or untracked worktree files (like
.Xr git 1 Cm ls-files ) .
Default prints tracked paths, sorted.
.Fl s
adds stage info
.Pq Ql <mode> <hash> <stage>\et<path> , No stage always 0 ;
.Fl Fl others
lists untracked files instead;
.Fl Fl exclude-standard
drops
.Pa .mkitignore Ns -ignored
files and
.Fl Fl ignored
shows only those
.Pq Fl Fl ignored No requires Fl Fl others ;
.Fl z
NUL-terminates records with raw paths.
.It Cm rev-parse Oo Fl Fl verify Oc Oo Fl Fl short Ns Op = Ns Ar N Oc Oo Fl Fl abbrev-ref Oc Oo Fl Fl show-toplevel Oc Op Ar rev ...
Resolve revisions to object ids.
.Fl Fl short
abbreviates (default 7, a BLAKE3 prefix);
.Fl Fl abbrev-ref Cm HEAD
prints the current branch;
.Fl Fl show-toplevel
prints the repository root.
.It Cm rev-list Oo Fl Fl count Oc Ar rev
List the commit ids reachable from
.Ar rev
in reverse-chronological order, one per line;
.Fl Fl count
prints the number instead.
.It Cm merge-base Oo Fl Fl is-ancestor Oc Ar a Ar b
Print the best common ancestor of two commits
.Pq exit 1, no output, when none .
.Fl Fl is-ancestor
tests whether
.Ar a
is an ancestor of
.Ar b ,
exiting 0 or 1 with no output.
.It Cm show-ref Oo Fl Fl heads Oc Op Fl Fl tags
List refs as
.Ql <hash> <refname> ,
sorted by name.
.Fl Fl heads Ns / Ns Fl Fl tags
filter to that namespace.
.It Cm for-each-ref Oo Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Ar fmt Oc Op Ar pattern ...
Iterate refs (heads and tags), sorted by name (like
.Xr git 1 Cm for-each-ref ) .
The default line is
.Ql <objectname> <objecttype>\et<refname> ;
.Fl Fl format
substitutes
.Ql %(atom)
tokens
.Pq Cm refname , refname:short , objectname , objectname:short , objecttype .
Optional
.Ar pattern
arguments filter by ref-name prefix.
.It Cm symbolic-ref Oo Fl Fl short Oc Ar name Op Ar ref
Read or write a symbolic ref (currently only
.Cm HEAD ) .
Without
.Ar ref ,
prints the target
.Pq Ql refs/heads/main , No or Ql main No with Fl Fl short ;
errors when HEAD is detached. With
.Ar ref
(which must be under
.Pa refs/heads/ ) ,
repoints HEAD at that branch \(em the plumbing form, which does not touch the
worktree; the branch need not exist yet.
.It Cm update-ref Oo Fl d Oc Ar ref Oo Ar newvalue Oo Ar oldvalue Oc Oc
Create, update, or delete a ref under
.Pa refs/heads/
or
.Pa refs/tags/ .
.Ar newvalue
and
.Ar oldvalue
resolve through the revspec grammar. With no
.Ar oldvalue
the write is unconditional; with one it is a compare-and-swap that fails
unless the ref currently holds that value. In update mode an all-zero
.Ar oldvalue
requires the ref to be absent (git's create-only form).
.Fl d
deletes the ref, optionally checking a
.Em concrete
.Ar oldvalue
first \(em an all-zero value is rejected in delete mode, since a ref asserted
absent has nothing to delete. Deleting a branch refuses the currently
checked-out one, an mkit safety divergence.
.It Cm commit Oo Fl a | Fl Fl all Oc Oo Fl Fl amend Oc Oo Fl m Ar message | Fl F Ar file Oc Op Fl Fl author Ar spec
Create a signed commit. With
.Fl a
or
.Fl Fl all ,
first stage modified tracked files and tracked deletions, but do not add
untracked files. The combined
.Fl am Ar message
form is accepted as shorthand for
.Fl a Fl m Ar message .
If neither
.Fl m
nor
.Fl F
is given,
.Nm
opens
.Ev EDITOR
on
.Pa .mkit/COMMIT_EDITMSG ;
lines starting with
.Ql #
are stripped, and an empty message aborts the commit.
.Fl F
or
.Fl Fl file
reads the commit message from
.Ar file
.Pq Ql - No reads from stdin
and is mutually exclusive with
.Fl m .
.Fl Fl author Ar spec
overrides the commit author Identity for this commit.
.Pp
After a
.Cm merge Fl Fl no-commit
.Pq or any otherwise-resolved merge ,
.Cm commit
records a two-parent merge commit from the staged merge result.
.Pp
With
.Fl Fl amend ,
replace the current commit instead of adding a new one: the new commit
re-uses HEAD's parent(s) as its own parent(s), takes its tree from the
staging index, is re-signed, and the branch is moved to it. If
.Fl m
is omitted the previous commit's message is reused and no editor is
launched. The superseded commit is not deleted; it remains on disk as an
unreachable object until
.Cm gc
ships
.Pq issue #233 .
.It Cm log Oo Fl Fl oneline Oc Oo Fl Fl abbrev-commit Oc Oo Fl Fl abbrev Ns = Ns Ar N Oc Oo Fl Fl format Ar json Oc Oo Fl Fl graph Oc Oo Fl n Ar N Oc Oo Ar rev | Ar A Ns .. Ns Ar B | Ar A Ns ... Ns Ar B Oc
Show commit history. With no argument the walk starts at
.Cm HEAD ;
an optional
.Ar rev
starts it there instead, and a range
.Ar A Ns .. Ns Ar B
shows commits reachable from
.Ar B
but not
.Ar A
(an empty side means
.Cm HEAD ) .
Commits are ordered reverse-chronologically with a topological tie-break (a
parent never precedes a child) \(em git's
.Fl Fl date-order ,
which matches git's default for linear and monotonic-timestamp history. An
.Ar A Ns ... Ns Ar B
symmetric range shows commits reachable from
.Ar A
or
.Ar B
but not their common ancestors. The default format prints the full commit message body,
indented by four spaces, and renders the timestamp as a stable UTC date in the
form
.Ql YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS +0000 .
.Fl Fl oneline
condenses each commit to
.Ql <abbrev-hex> <title> .
.Fl Fl abbrev-commit
abbreviates the commit id in the default format too, and
.Fl Fl abbrev Ns = Ns Ar N
sets the abbreviation length (bare
.Fl Fl abbrev
and
.Fl Fl oneline
default to 7, clamped to the range 4 to 64). The abbreviated id is a BLAKE3
prefix, so it is longer than a Git SHA-1 prefix of the same length.
.Fl Fl format Ar json
emits one JSON object per commit on stdout, keeping the raw integer Unix
timestamp for machine consumption.
.Fl Fl graph
is accepted for compatibility but is currently a no-op (no ASCII graph is
drawn).
.It Cm reflog Oo Ar ref Oc Oo Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json Oc Op Fl n Ar N
Show a branch's recorded movement history, read-only. Walks the branch
tip's first-parent chain newest-first, addressing each entry
.Ar ref Ns Cm @{ Ns Ar N Ns Cm } ,
where
.Cm @{0}
is the current tip. Defaults to the branch
.Cm HEAD
points at; a detached
.Cm HEAD
needs an explicit
.Ar ref
because the ref-history journal is keyed per-branch.
.Pp
The underlying journal is the per-branch, append-only commit-history MMR
written by every branch advance. It stores a count of advances and a
tamper-evident root, but its leaf digests cannot be decoded back to
commit hashes, so the listed hashes are reconstructed from the
first-parent chain. On a build with
.Cm --features history-mmr
each listed entry is additionally cross-checked against the journaled
MMR root via an inclusion proof. This is
.Em not
a full Git reflog:
.Cm @{ Ns Ar N Ns Cm }
indexes the reachable chain, so commits superseded by
.Cm commit --amend
or a reset are no longer listed. This command never mutates refs, the
journal, or any object.
.It Cm diff Oo Fl Fl staged | Fl Fl cached Oc Oo Fl Fl name-only | Fl Fl name-status | Fl Fl stat Oc Oo Fl Fl merge-base Oc Oo Fl z Oc Oo Ar rev Op Ar rev | Ar a Ns .. Ns Ar b | Ar a Ns ... Ns Ar b Oc Ar path ...
Show changes as a unified patch. With no arguments, compares the HEAD tree to
a fresh worktree snapshot.
.Fl Fl staged
.Pq alias Fl Fl cached
compares the HEAD tree to the staged index tree. A single revision compares
that revision against the worktree; two revisions, or an
.Ar a Ns .. Ns Ar b
range, diff the two resolved trees directly. A symmetric
.Ar a Ns ... Ns Ar b
range diffs the merge base of
.Ar a
and
.Ar b
against
.Ar b .
.Fl Fl merge-base
is the flag spelling of an
.Ar a Ns ... Ns Ar b
range: it diffs the merge base of the given revisions instead of the
revisions directly.
Revisions may be refs, commit
hashes, short hashes, or
.Ql HEAD~n
(a raw 64-hex tree hash also works). Remaining positional arguments are
pathspecs that limit the output.
.Fl Fl name-only
lists one changed path per line;
.Fl Fl name-status
prefixes each with an
.Ql A Ns / Ns Ql D Ns / Ns Ql M
status letter
.Pq Ql T marks an mkit mode change .
.Fl Fl stat
shows a diffstat: a padded
.Ql <name> | <count> <graph>
row per file (the
.Ql +/-
graph scaled to the terminal width, honoring
.Ev COLUMNS ,
default 80) and a
.Ql N files changed, ... insertions(+), ... deletions(-)
summary; binary files render
.Ql Bin <old> -> <new> bytes .
In these summary modes special-byte paths are C-style quoted (git
.Ql core.quotePath ) ;
.Fl z
instead NUL-terminates records and emits raw paths, and is only valid with
.Fl Fl name-only
or
.Fl Fl name-status .
.It Cm status Oo Fl Fl porcelain Ns Oo = Ns Cm v1 | Cm v2 Oc Oc Oo Fl s | Fl Fl short Oc Oo Fl z Oc
Show the staged and working-tree changes.
.Fl s
.Pq alias Fl Fl short
is equivalent to
.Fl Fl porcelain Ns = Ns Cm v1 :
both emit machine-readable XY-code-plus-path lines on stdout. Paths with
special bytes are C-style quoted (matching git's default
.Cm core.quotePath ) .
.Fl z
.Pq which implies porcelain
NUL-terminates records and emits raw, unquoted paths.
.Fl Fl porcelain Ns = Ns Cm v2
emits git's richer per-path format \(em
.Ql 1 <XY> <sub> <mH> <mI> <mW> <hH> <hI> <path>
for tracked changes (octal modes, full object ids; mkit emits no rename
.Ql 2
lines and is never a submodule, so
.Ar sub
is always
.Ql N... ) ,
and
.Ql ? <path>
for untracked. Header lines
.Pq Fl Fl branch
are not emitted.
.It Cm branch Oo Fl v | Fl Fl verbose Oc Oo Fl d | Fl D | Fl m Oc Oo Fl Fl list Oc Oo Fl Fl contains Ar commit Oc Oo Fl Fl no-contains Ar commit Oc Oo Fl Fl merged Op Ar commit Oc Oo Fl Fl no-merged Op Ar commit Oc Op Ar name ...
List, create, rename, or delete branches. With no arguments, lists
branches as
.Ql <marker> <name>
with no commit id, like
.Xr git 1 Cm branch ;
.Ql * Ns \&
marks the current branch.
.Fl v
.Pq Fl Fl verbose
adds the abbreviated tip id and commit subject (a BLAKE3 prefix, not a
40-hex SHA-1 prefix). With one
.Ar name ,
creates a branch at HEAD.
.Fl d
deletes a branch (refuses the current branch);
.Fl D
force-deletes. Both error on an absent branch (like
.Xr git 1 Cm branch Fl D ) ,
and both still refuse the current branch.
.Fl m Oo Ar old Oc Ar new
renames a branch \(em the current branch when
.Ar old
is omitted \(em and moves HEAD if the renamed branch is checked out.
.Pp
The listing accepts ancestry filters (like
.Xr git 1 Cm branch ) :
.Fl Fl contains Ar commit
keeps only branches whose tip has
.Ar commit
as an ancestor, and
.Fl Fl no-contains Ar commit
inverts that;
.Fl Fl merged Op Ar commit
keeps branches already merged into
.Ar commit
and
.Fl Fl no-merged Op Ar commit
keeps those not yet merged, both defaulting
.Ar commit
to
.Cm HEAD .
.Fl Fl list
is an explicit list selector.
.It Cm checkout Ar branch
Switch HEAD to a branch and restore files from its tree.
Prints
.Sy Switched to branch Ar n
or
.Sy Already on Ar n
for a no-op switch.
.It Cm checkout Oo Fl b | Fl B Oc Ar new Op Ar start
Create
.Pq Fl b , or create-or-reset Fl B
a branch at
.Ar start
.Pq default HEAD
and switch to it.
.It Cm switch Ar branch
Switch branches (git
.Cm switch ) .
A thin front-end over
.Cm checkout
with the same clobber guard and output.
.It Cm switch Oo Fl c | Fl C Oc Ar new Op Ar start
Create
.Pq Fl c , or create-or-reset Fl C
a branch and switch to it.
.It Cm tag Oo Fl a | Fl s Oc Oo Fl m Ar msg Oc Oo Fl Fl author Ar spec Oc Oo Ar name Oc Op Ar commit
List, create, or delete tags. With no arguments, prints the list of
tags, marking each annotated or signed tag. With a
.Ar name
argument and no flags, creates a lightweight tag pointing straight at
.Ar commit
.Pq default HEAD .
.Fl a
creates an annotated tag object (name, tagger identity, message,
timestamp);
.Fl s
creates a signed annotated tag whose 64-byte field is an Ed25519
signature over the canonical tag bytes under the distinct
.Sy mkit.tag
domain.
.Fl m
supplies the message; without it
.Ev EDITOR
is launched.
.Fl Fl author Ar spec
overrides the tagger Identity for an annotated or signed tag.
.Fl d
deletes the named tag. Verify a signed tag with
.Cm mkit verify Ar name .
.It Cm sparse-checkout Cm set | Cm list | Cm disable | Cm reapply Op Ar pattern ...
Manage the sparse-checkout pattern set at
.Pa .mkit/sparse-checkout .
.Cm set
overwrites the pattern list,
.Cm list
prints the current patterns,
.Cm disable
clears them, and
.Cm reapply
re-materialises the working tree from the current patterns.
.It Cm merge Oo Fl Fl no-commit Oc Oo Fl m Ar msg Oc Ar branch | Fl Fl continue | Fl Fl abort
Merge
.Ar branch
into HEAD. Fast-forwards when possible.
.Fl Fl no-commit
stages the merge result but stops before committing; finish with
.Cm mkit commit
or
.Cm mkit merge Fl Fl continue
.Pq fast-forwards are unaffected by this flag .
.Fl m
or
.Fl Fl message
overrides the merge commit message. After a conflict, resolve and
.Cm add
the paths, then
.Fl Fl continue ,
or
.Fl Fl abort
to restore HEAD.
.It Cm rebase Oo Fl i Oc Ar branch
Replay commits from HEAD onto
.Ar branch .
With
.Fl i
.Pq Fl Fl interactive
the todo list opens in
.Ev $EDITOR
first: reorder lines to reorder commits, delete a line (or mark it
.Cm drop )
to drop a commit, mark a line
.Cm reword
to edit that commit's message during replay, or fold a commit into the
previous one with
.Cm squash
(combine messages) or
.Cm fixup
(keep the previous message). A
.Cm squash Ns / Ns Cm fixup
cannot be the first line and is rejected before any mutation. Removing every
line resets the branch to the base.
.Cm edit
(stop-to-amend) is not yet supported.
Accepts
.Fl Fl continue ,
.Fl Fl skip ,
and
.Fl Fl abort
after a conflict.
.It Cm push Op Fl Fl dry-run
Push HEAD's commit chain to the configured remote.
.It Cm pull , Cm fetch
Download or download-and-merge from the remote.
.It Cm clone Op Fl Fl sparse ... Ar url
Clone a remote into a new working directory.
.It Cm remote Oo Cm add | Cm set | Cm remove | Cm rename Oc Op Ar args ...
Show or configure remotes. With no subcommand, lists the configured
remotes.
.Cm add
(alias
.Cm set )
adds the flat default remote
.Pq Cm remote add Ar url
or a named remote
.Pq Cm remote add Ar name url ;
URLs MUST use the strict
.Ql mkit+<scheme>://
form.
.Cm remove Ar name
.Pq alias Cm rm
deletes a named remote
.Pq the reserved Ar default No name clears the flat remote ;
.Cm rename Ar old new
.Pq alias Cm mv
renames a named remote and repoints any branch upstream tracking it.
Removing or renaming a remote never touches the user-scoped
.Ar trusted_remote_endpoint ,
which is keyed by exact URL rather than remote name.
.It Cm cherry-pick Oo Fl n | Fl Fl no-commit Oc Oo Fl m Ar parent-number Oc Ar hash | Fl Fl continue | Fl Fl abort
Apply a single commit from elsewhere onto HEAD.
.Fl n
or
.Fl Fl no-commit
stages the picked change in the index and worktree without creating a
commit.
.Fl m
or
.Fl Fl mainline Ar parent-number
selects which parent of a merge commit is the mainline (git semantics):
it is required when replaying a merge commit and rejected for a non-merge.
This differs from
.Xr git 1 Ns 's
.Cm commit Fl m :
git's
.Cm cherry-pick Fl m
is mainline selection, not a message override.
On conflict, resolve and
.Cm add
the paths, then
.Fl Fl continue ,
or
.Fl Fl abort
to restore HEAD.
.It Cm revert Ar commit | Fl Fl continue | Fl Fl abort Oo Fl n | Fl Fl no-commit Oc
Create a new commit that undoes
.Ar commit
(the inverse of cherry-pick). A normal forward commit — it does not rewrite
history, so the reverted commit stays reachable. On conflict the resolvable
workflow applies:
.Fl Fl continue
after
.Cm add Ns -ing
resolutions, or
.Fl Fl abort
to restore HEAD.
.Fl n
.Pq Fl Fl no-commit
stages the reverted tree without committing. Reverting a merge commit is
refused (mainline selection is not yet supported).
.It Cm bisect Cm start | Cm good | Cm bad | Cm reset
Binary-search for the commit that introduced a regression.
.It Cm gc Oo Fl n | Fl Fl dry-run Oc Oo Fl Fl grace-secs Ar secs Oc
Reclaim unreachable objects (mark-and-sweep). Keeps everything reachable from
the retention roots (refs, stash, in-progress operations, attestations, and the
recovery log), expires stale recovery entries, then deletes unreachable objects
older than the grace window
.Pq default 14 days .
.Fl n
.Pq Fl Fl dry-run
previews without deleting.
.Fl Fl grace-secs Ar 0
prunes every unreachable object, but bypasses the grace window — that window is
gc's concurrency-safety net (some root publishers such as
.Cm tag
and
.Cm fetch
write an object before publishing its ref), so
.Fl Fl grace-secs Ar 0
must be run only when no other mkit process is operating on the repo (gc prints
a warning). Fails closed: a corrupt root or unreadable ref aborts with nothing
removed.
.It Cm stash Oo Cm save Fl m Ar msg | Cm list | Cm pop Oo Fl Fl index Oc Op Ar ref | Cm apply Oo Fl Fl index Oc Op Ar ref | Cm drop Op Ar ref | Cm clear | Cm show Op Ar ref Oc
Save or restore a snapshot of working-tree changes.
.Cm pop
applies an entry and removes it;
.Cm apply
applies an entry but leaves it on the stack. Both run the
destructive-restore guard so uncommitted edits on unrelated paths are
never clobbered.
.Fl Fl index
on
.Cm pop Ns / Ns Cm apply
also restores the staged state recorded when the stash was created (like
.Xr git 1
.Cm stash pop Fl Fl index ) ,
not just the worktree changes.
.Cm save
records that staged snapshot as the stash commit's second parent, so it
needs no on-disk format change; stashes created before this feature carry
no index snapshot and
.Fl Fl index
is a no-op for them.
.Cm clear
removes every stash entry.
.Cm pop , Cm apply , Cm drop ,
and
.Cm show
take an entry selector
.Ar ref
.Pq default 0
that may be either a bare index
.Ar N
or the
.Cm stash@{ Ns Ar N Ns Cm }
form.
.It Cm blame Oo Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json Oc Op Fl w Op Fl M Op Fl C Oo Fl Fl ignore-rev Ar rev Oc Op Fl Fl first-parent Op Fl Fl reverse Oo Fl L Ar start , end Oc Op Ar rev Ar file
Line-level attribution of
.Ar file
as of
.Ar rev
.Pq a ref, hash, or Cm HEAD~2 Ns -style spec; default Cm HEAD .
.Fl w
ignores whitespace when matching lines across revisions
.Pq like Xr git 1 Cm blame Fl w , ignoring all whitespace ,
so a whitespace-only edit does not reattribute the line; output still
shows the file's current bytes.
.Fl M
detects lines moved within the file and
.Fl C
lines copied from other files
.Pq like Xr git 1 Cm blame Fl M Ns / Ns Fl C ; Fl C No implies Fl M ,
and repeating
.Fl C
widens the search from files changed in the commit to every file in the
parent. Detection is block-based: the longest contiguous block above git's
default thresholds (20 alphanumeric characters for
.Fl M ,
40 for
.Fl C )
is credited to its origin, so a moved block beside new lines is still split
out and, with
.Fl w ,
a block copied with a whitespace change is still detected. git's inline
numeric forms
.Pq Fl M Ns Ar num , Fl C Ns Ar num
are not exposed.
.Fl Fl ignore-rev Ar rev
.Pq repeatable
and
.Fl Fl ignore-revs-file Ar file
skip
.Dq noise
commits during attribution
.Pq like Xr git 1 Cm blame Fl Fl ignore-rev :
a line that would be credited to an ignored commit falls through to the
commit that previously changed it, while a line the commit genuinely
inserted stays put.
.Fl Fl ignore-rev
accepts any revision; the file holds one full hex object name per line,
skipping blank lines and
.Sq #
comments.
.Fl L
restricts output to a line range:
.Ar start , end ,
.Ar start , Ns Cm + Ns Ar n
.Pq n lines forward from start ,
.Ar start , Ns Cm - Ns Ar n
.Pq n lines back, ending at start ,
.Ar start ,
.Pq to end of file ,
.Cm \&, Ns Ar end
.Pq from start of file ,
or a bare
.Ar start
.Pq to end of file .
Bounds are 1-based and inclusive; an inverted range is swapped and an
over-long end is clamped to the last line, matching
.Xr git 1 Cm blame .
Blame is merge-aware by default
.Pq like Xr git 1 Cm blame :
at a merge a line is credited to whichever parent's side wrote it, so a line
merged in from a side branch is attributed to its authoring commit rather
than the merge.
.Fl Fl first-parent
.Pq like Xr git 1 Cm blame Fl Fl first-parent
follows only each commit's first parent, crediting such a line to the merge
commit instead.
.Fl Fl reverse Ar start Ns Cm .. Ns Ar end
walks history forward instead of backward
.Pq like Xr git 1 Cm blame Fl Fl reverse :
it blames the
.Ar start
version of the file and attributes each line to the last commit in the
range in which it still existed.
.Ar start Ns Cm ..
defaults
.Ar end
to
.Cm HEAD ;
an explicit
.Ar start
is required.
A line that never survives a step stays on
.Ar start
.Pq Xr git 1 prints such lines with a leading Ql ^ ; No mkit's tab format has none .
.Fl w
and
.Fl L
compose with
.Fl Fl reverse .
The default output is
.Ql <short12>\et<line_num>\et<text>
per line;
.Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json
instead emits JSONL with
.Cm hash , line_num , author , timestamp ,
and
.Cm text
keys.
.It Cm verify Ar rev
Verify the signature on a commit, remix, or signed tag.
.Ar rev
is an object hash, a branch or tag name, or
.Cm HEAD ;
a tag name resolves to its annotated-tag object when one exists.
.It Cm attest Oo Fl Fl commit Ar hash Oc Op Fl Fl algorithm Ar alg Op Fl Fl signer Ar kind Op Fl Fl predicate-type Ar uri Op Fl Fl predicate-file Ar path
Produce a signed DSSE attestation for the given commit (defaults to
HEAD). Multiple
.Fl Fl additional-signer
specs may be supplied to co-sign the attestation. The resulting
envelope is attached to the commit's attestation set.
.Fl Fl external-signer-arg Ar V
.Pq repeatable
passes an argument to an external signer; if any instance is supplied,
the full list
.Em replaces
.Cm attest.external_signer_args
from config rather than appending to it.
.It Cm verify-attest Oo Fl Fl commit Ar hash Oc Op Fl Fl trust-roots Ar path Op Fl Fl algorithm Ar filter
Verify every attestation attached to the given commit. Returns success
only if every attached envelope verifies.
.Fl Fl algorithm
filters which signature lines are
.Em displayed
to the named algorithm; it does NOT change the pass/fail criterion — every
envelope must still verify regardless of the filter, so it cannot be used to
require that a specific algorithm signed.
.It Cm key Cm generate | Cm list | Cm import | Cm export | Cm delete
Manage user-scoped keystore keys backed by the platform keystore
(Keychain on macOS, libsecret/systemd-creds/YubiKey on Linux, Credential
Manager on Windows). Unlike
.Cm keygen
(which writes to a repo-local file), these keys persist across repos.
.It Cm keygen Oo Fl Fl algorithm Cm ed25519 | Cm secp256k1 | Cm p256 Oc Oo Fl Fl force Oc Op Fl Fl print-pubkey
Generate a fresh keypair.
.Fl Fl algorithm
selects the key type
.Pq default Cm ed25519 ;
the destination depends on it:
.Cm ed25519
writes the signing key to the
.Cm signing_key
path
.Pq default Pa .mkit/keys/default.key ,
while
.Cm secp256k1
and
.Cm p256
write attestation keys to the
.Cm attest. Ns Ar algo Ns Cm _key_path
paths
.Pq default Pa .mkit/keys/secp256k1.key No / Pa .mkit/keys/p256.key .
.Fl Fl force
overwrites an existing key file at the target path; and
.Fl Fl print-pubkey
emits the canonical keyid on stdout for trust-roots entries.
.It Cm config Oo Ar key Oo Ar value Oc Oc Op Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json
Read or write a config key. With no
.Ar key ,
shows every configuration value; with a
.Ar key
alone, shows that value; with a
.Ar key Ar value
pair, sets it. On the show forms,
.Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm json
emits JSON instead of the default plain output. Recognized keys include
.Cm user.identity , Cm ssh.strict_host_key_checking ,
.Cm ssh.user_known_hosts_file ,
and
.Cm ssh.identity_file .
The git-compatibility aliases
.Cm user.name
and
.Cm user.email
are accepted and round-tripped but are
.Em non-authoritative :
they never set the signed commit author (that is
.Cm user.identity
or the signing key), so they are repo-safe, unlike
.Cm user.identity .
.It Cm serve Ar path
Internal subcommand: act as the server end of an
.Cm mkit+ssh://
transport.
.It Cm mcp Op Fl Fl repository Ar path
Start a Model Context Protocol server on stdio so LLM agents can drive
local repositories: status, diff, log, show, branch, add, commit,
checkout, plus signature verification and DSSE attestation tools.
.Fl Fl repository
confines every tool call to the given path. The server runs no network
operations and never overrides
.Nm Ns 's
data-loss guards.
.It Cm pack-shard Oo Fl Fl out Ar dir Oc Oo Fl Fl force Oc Ar hash
Encode a stored pack into Reed-Solomon shards plus a manifest, ready to
publish to an HTTP or S3 origin (producer side of SPEC-PACK-SHARDS Phase
2). The pack must already be in the local object store. Output lands
under
.Pa <out>/packs/<hex>/shards/<index>
with the manifest written last, so racing readers see either no manifest
(clean fall-through to the monolithic pack) or the manifest plus every
shard.
.Fl Fl out Ar dir
sets the output directory
.Pq default Pa .mkit/pack-shards ;
.Fl Fl force
encodes even when the pack is below the size threshold. Requires building
the binary with
.Cm --features pack-shards .
.It Cm git Cm export Oo Fl Fl remote-name Ar name Oc Oo Fl Fl ref Ar ref Oc Oo Fl Fl passthrough Oc Oo Fl Fl no-attest Oc Oo Fl Fl algorithm Ar alg Oc Oo Fl Fl signer Ar kind Oc Oo Fl Fl json Oc Ar dest
Deterministic one-way export of branches and tags to a git mirror per
SPEC-GIT-BRIDGE. mkit-only fields ride in
.Ql mkit-*
commit headers so the original signed objects are reconstructible;
untranslatable refs are skipped per-ref with a warning. Pushes use
per-ref
.Fl Fl force-with-lease
from state recorded under
.Pa .mkit/git/<name>/ .
The default
.Fl Fl remote-name
is
.Ql mirror ;
.Fl Fl ref
takes full names
.Pq Ql refs/heads/... , Ql refs/tags/... .
Unless
.Fl Fl no-attest ,
each exported head gets a signed git-bridge/v1 DSSE attestation
(signer resolution as in
.Cm mkit attest :
.Fl Fl algorithm / Fl Fl signer
flags, else config defaults),
published on the mirror's
.Ql refs/mkit/attestations
ref. With
.Fl Fl passthrough
(fork mode, SPEC-GIT-BRIDGE \(sc14), an imported repository publishes
as a true git fork: imported objects re-emit their original sha1s and
only native commits translate, so merge bases with the upstream exist
and pull requests work. The import direction is
.Cm mkit git import
(SPEC-GIT-IMPORT); there is no bidirectional sync. Requires building
the binary with
.Cm --features git-bridge
and a
.Cm git
binary on
.Ev PATH .
.It Cm git Cm import Oo Fl Fl remote-name Ar name Oc Oo Fl Fl key Ar path Oc Oo Fl Fl json Oc Ar url Op Ar dir
Import a git upstream as an importer-signed downstream fork per
SPEC-GIT-IMPORT. With
.Ar dir ,
initialise a fresh repository and check out the upstream default
branch; without, add tracking refs to the current repository. Signed
by a dedicated import key, pinned per state dir.
.It Cm git Cm fetch | Cm pull Oo Fl Fl remote-name Ar name Oc Oo Fl Fl key Ar path Oc Op Fl Fl json
Update
.Pa refs/remotes/<name>/*
and imported tags from the upstream (a locally-moved tag is never
clobbered);
.Cm pull
also fast-forwards the current branch (divergence refuses with a
.Cm mkit merge
hint).
.Fl Fl key Ar path
selects the import signing key
.Pq default the pinned key file ,
and
.Fl Fl json
emits machine-readable JSON on stdout.
.It Cm git Cm verify Oo Fl Fl remote-name Ar name Oc Oo Fl Fl fork-audit Oc Oo Fl Fl ref Ar ref Oc
Audit recorded bridge state against the local store: bridge-translated
objects shallow-verify and must reconstruct to their mapped twin;
imported objects must have retained raw bytes hashing to their sha1
and a twin signed by the pinned importer key.
.Fl Fl fork-audit
additionally re-derives every tree and blob referenced by bridge
commits (SPEC-GIT-BRIDGE \(sc14.3). Exits non-zero listing each
failing object; unsigned (all-zero-signature) objects also fail,
reported as a count.
.It Cm git Cm status
Show every bridge state dir: direction, recorded endpoints, pinned
importer key, tracking and lease ref positions, staging health.
.It Cm git Cm format-patch Oo Fl o Ar dir Oc Oo Fl Fl stdout Oc Ar range
Render native commits in
.Ar range
.Pq Ql A..B , or Ql rev for Ql rev..HEAD
as
.Cm git am Ns
-able mbox patches, oldest first; merge commits are skipped.
.It Cm self Cm update Oo Fl Fl version Ar tag Oc Oo Fl Fl check Oc Oo Fl Fl allow-downgrade Oc Oo Fl Fl format Ar human|json Oc
Update this binary in place from a signed GitHub Release. The
downloaded archive is verified against the mkit-native release
attestation (a DSSE/in-toto envelope over the archives' BLAKE3
digests) using release-attestation public keys embedded in the binary
at build time \(em no
.Cm cosign
or GitHub attestation API required. Only installer-managed binaries
(installed via
.Ql curl mkit.sh | sh ,
which writes the
.Pa .mkit-installed-tag
receipt) are updated; Homebrew and cargo installs are refused with
channel-specific guidance instead.
.Fl Fl check
only reports whether an update is available and changes nothing.
Resolving
.Ql latest
never downgrades; an explicit
.Fl Fl version
pin may only downgrade together with
.Fl Fl allow-downgrade .
Receipts
.Pq Pa .mkit-installed-tag , MKIT_STATE_DIR/installed-tag
are rewritten after a successful swap, in the installer's format.
There is no background update check; the command only ever acts when
invoked. Not yet supported on Windows.
.It Cm version
Print the version to stdout as
.Ql mkit <X.Y.Z> .
The top-level
.Fl Fl version
and
.Fl V
flags are aliases of this subcommand and emit the identical string. Note this
is
.Ql mkit <X.Y.Z> ,
not Git's
.Ql git version <X.Y.Z>
form \(em an intentional, packaging-pinned divergence.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "CLICOLOR_FORCE"
.It Ev GIT_EDITOR , Ev EDITOR , Ev VISUAL
Editor opened by
.Cm mkit commit
when neither
.Fl m
nor
.Fl F
is supplied, tried in that order. If none is set it falls back to
.Xr vi 1
(Unix) or
.Ic notepad
(Windows), matching git. In a headless or non-interactive context, pass
.Fl m
or
.Fl F
so the commit never blocks on an editor.
.It Ev NO_COLOR
If set to any value (including empty),
.Nm
suppresses ANSI color output on stdout. See
.Lk https://no-color.org .
.It Ev CLICOLOR_FORCE
If set to
.Ql 1 ,
.Nm
emits ANSI color even when stdout is not a terminal.
.Ev NO_COLOR
overrides this.
.It Ev SSH_AUTH_SOCK
Standard OpenSSH agent socket; used when pushing or cloning via
.Cm mkit+ssh:// .
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME , Ev XDG_DATA_HOME , Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME , Ev XDG_STATE_HOME
Locations of user-level config, keystore, cache, and state
respectively. See
.Sx FILES .
.It Ev GH_TOKEN , Ev GITHUB_TOKEN
Bearer token
.Cm mkit self update
sends to the GitHub API, tried in that order. Optional for public
releases; required while the repository is private.
.It Ev MKIT_STATE_DIR
Receipt state dir consulted by
.Cm mkit self update
(default
.Pa ~/.local/state/mkit ) ,
matching the installer's variable of the same name.
.It Ev MKIT_SELF_UPDATE_API_BASE
Override the release-API base URL used by
.Cm mkit self update
(default
.Lk https://api.github.com/repos/officialunofficial/mkit ) .
For tests and mirrors.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "~/.local/state/mkit/"
.It Pa .mkit/
Repo-local state dir (analogous to
.Pa .git/ ).
.It Pa .mkit/config
Repo-local config; overrides user-level values for ordinary keys. Security-
sensitive keys
.Pq Cm signing_key , Cm user.identity , Cm signer , Cm key.* , Cm attest.* , Cm ssh.* , No and Cm trusted_remote_endpoint
are user-scoped only and ignored here.
.It Pa .mkit/keys/default.key
Repo-local Ed25519 signing key.
.It Pa .mkit/index
Staged-change index.
.It Pa .mkit/index.lock
Lockfile held by
.Cm commit , Cm checkout , Cm merge , Cm rebase
to serialize concurrent invocations.
.It Pa .mkit/COMMIT_EDITMSG
Scratch file written before
.Ev EDITOR
is invoked by
.Cm commit .
.It Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mkit/config
User-level config.
.It Pa $XDG_DATA_HOME/mkit/keys/
User-level keystore (optional; for a single identity across repos).
.It Pa $XDG_CACHE_HOME/mkit/
User-level cache directory.
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/mkit/
User-level state directory.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
follows the BSD
.Xr sysexits 3
conventions where practical:
.Bl -tag -width "76"
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
General error (catch-all).
.It 64
Command-line usage error (unknown subcommand, missing required arg).
.It 65
Input data was malformed (corrupt object, bad hex hash).
.It 66
Input file was missing or unreadable.
.It 69
Service unavailable (transport could not connect).
.It 73
Could not create output (permission denied writing a ref, etc.).
.It 75
Temporary failure; retrying is safe (5xx after retry budget,
SIGINT/SIGTERM interruption).
.It 76
Protocol error (bad URL scheme, malformed server response).
.It 77
Permission denied.
.It 78
Config error (unknown key, invalid value).
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Errors are written to stderr on a single line beginning with
.Ql error: .
When
.Ev NO_COLOR
is unset and stdout is a terminal, colored labels may be used; consult
.Ev CLICOLOR_FORCE
to override.
.Pp
.Nm
installs
.Dv SIGPIPE
as ignored, so common pipelines like
.Dl mkit log | head -1
exit cleanly without a "Broken pipe" message.
.Dv SIGINT
and
.Dv SIGTERM
set a flag that long-running operations honor at the next checkpoint.
